{
  "_comment": [
    "",
    "Factory ammunition, as published by the manufacturer.",
    "",
    "EVERY FIGURE HERE IS ADVERTISED, NOT MEASURED. Two consequences the app",
    "has to keep visible rather than paper over:",
    "",
    "  * Muzzle velocity comes from the maker's test barrel, usually 24in.",
    "    A shorter hunting barrel loses roughly 20-30 fps per inch, so a 20in",
    "    rifle can easily be 100 fps down on the box. That is inches of drop",
    "    at 400 yards and it moves the max ethical range the wrong way.",
    "    Chronograph your own rifle and override the figure if you can.",
    "  * Advertised ballistic coefficients tend to be optimistic.",
    "",
    "A ballistic coefficient is meaningless without the drag model it was",
    "measured against, so there is no bare `bc` field - only `bc_g1` and",
    "`bc_g7`. At least one must be present. The app prefers G7 where it",
    "exists, because these are boat-tail hunting bullets and G7 fits them",
    "better; using a G1 number with the G7 drag function (or the reverse)",
    "produces a plausible-looking trajectory that is simply wrong.",
    "",
    "BC is a property of the bullet, not the cartridge, so the same",
    "projectile loaded in two cartridges shares its BC. Velocity does not.",
    "",
    "`test_barrel_in` is null where the maker does not state it. Null means",
    "unknown, and the app says so rather than assuming 24in.",
    "",
    "Every entry records where its numbers came from and when. Keep that up",
    "when adding loads: catalogues change yearly, and a figure with no",
    "provenance cannot be rechecked.",
    "",
    "`stated_muzzle_energy_ft_lb` is the maker's own energy figure, carried",
    "purely as a cross-check and never used to compute anything. Energy is",
    "fixed by bullet weight and velocity, so a stated figure that disagrees",
    "with those two means one of them was typed wrong - and a wrong velocity",
    "is otherwise invisible, since it yields a trajectory that looks entirely",
    "reasonable. Fill it in wherever the maker publishes it; a test checks it",
    "to within 1.5%."
  ],
